; expect 45
; Macro definition and expansion in one file. `(macro (name params...)
; template)` is the real grammar - the name inside the head parens like
; a function's - and stage1 used to expect a spelling that appears
; nowhere in the language, so every macro was a parse error.
;
; Expansion is exercised where it can go wrong: nested invocation
; (`twice` inside `twice`, expanded by re-entering dispatch), a macro
; argument that is itself a call, expansion inside a `let` initialiser
; and an `if` arm, and a parameter used twice in its template - the
; double evaluation textual substitution has always meant.
; twice(twice(10)) = 40, plus (if true 5 0) via when = 45.
(macro (twice x) (+ x x))
(macro (when t b) (if t b 0))

(:: main Int)
(fn (main)
  (let ((n (twice (twice 10))))
    (+ n (when (== n 40) 5))))
